## 3️⃣  Alternative – In‑place **quick‑sort** (if you prefer a mutable algorithm)

If you need an **in‑place** version that does not allocate extra slices, the following implementation works for any `Comparable` type. It uses the classic Lomuto partition scheme.

```ql
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   In‑place QuickSort (Quelvin)
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
fn quickSort<T: Comparable>(arr: []T) -> void {
    fn partition(lo: int, hi: int) -> int {
        var pivot = arr[hi];               // choose last element as pivot
        var i = lo - 1;

        for var j = lo; j < hi; j += 1 {
            if arr[j].compare(pivot) <= 0 {
                i += 1;
                // swap arr[i] and arr[j]
                var tmp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[j];
                arr[j] = tmp;
            }
        }
        // place pivot after the last smaller element
        var tmp = arr[i + 1];
        arr[i + 1] = arr[hi];
        arr[hi] = tmp;
        return i + 1;
    }

    fn qs(lo: int, hi: int) -> void {
        if lo < hi {
            var p = partition(lo, hi);
            qs(lo, p - 1);
            qs(p + 1, hi);
        }
    }

    qs(0, arr.len() - 1);
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   Test harness – sorts an array of strings
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
impl Comparable for string {
    fn compare(other: string) -> int {
        // built‑in lexical compare, returns -1/0/1
        return self <=> other;   // syntactic sugar for three‑way compare
    }
}

fn main() -> void {
    var words: []string = ["pear", "apple", "orange", "banana"];
    println("Before: ", words);
    quickSort(words);
    println("After : ", words);
}
```

Both `mergeSort` (stable, O(n log n) extra memory) **and** `quickSort` (in‑place, O(log n) stack) are idiomatic ways to sort data in Quelvin. Choose the one that best matches your performance and memory constraints.

